DECnet for the Amiga
====================

A DECnet Phase IV endnode for AmigaOS 2.04 and newer, 68000 and up.
The Amiga becomes a node in a DECnet network with VAX/VMS, PDP-11 or
other DECnet systems: it fetches and stores files on them (DAP/FAL),
answers NCP LOOP NODE, and shows their files as a disk named DN:.


Requirements
------------
- AmigaOS 2.04 (V37) or newer, any 68k CPU
- an Ethernet card with a SANA-II driver (e.g. A2065), the same driver
  AmiTCP or Roadshow use
- a DECnet address (area.node) that is free in your network


Installation
------------
Double-click "Install-DECnet". It asks for the DECnet address and name
of this Amiga and the network driver, tests the card with dncheck,
copies the files and adds DECnet to S:User-Startup, in front of your
TCP/IP software. From the Shell: cd to this drawer, then

  Installer Install-DECnet MINUSER AVERAGE

DECnet must start before TCP/IP: a SANA-II card is configured only once
per boot, and DECnet needs the Ethernet address AA-00-04-00-xx-xx that
follows from its DECnet address. TCP/IP then uses this address as well;
if your DHCP server hands out a fixed IP address by Ethernet address,
change that entry. Reboot after the installation.

Miami (and other software that starts the network from
S:Startup-Sequence) comes too early for DECnet: Startup-Sequence runs
S:User-Startup only near its end. The installer warns when it finds
such a line, but it never changes S:Startup-Sequence. Do it yourself:
make a copy first, keep a boot disk at hand, then move the line that
starts the network (e.g. "Execute S/MIAMITCP-Startup") below the lines

  IF EXISTS S:User-Startup
    Execute S:User-Startup
  EndIF

so that DECnet, which starts in S:User-Startup, comes first.

Two programs show their output in a window of their own:

  DECnet-Check  dncheck's test of the card. The installation goes on
                when you close this window.
  DECnet-Nodes  only if you let the installer copy node names: the
                names dnnodes found. The installation does not wait
                for it; close it whenever you have read it.

AmigaOS 2.04 has no Installer and no LhA. Get them from Aminet first:
util/misc/Installer-43_3.lha (copy Installer to C:) and util/arc/lha.run
(run it, copy lha_68k to C:LhA). On 2.04 the text icons open More;
pictures need a viewer of your own (MultiView comes with OS 3.0).

To remove DECnet, start Install-DECnet again and choose "Remove". It
deletes only the files listed below (and DECnet-Term, if installed);
your own files in the DECnet drawer and ENVARC:DECnet/nodes stay.


What goes where
---------------
  DECnet/C/dnetd           the DECnet stack (started by DECnet-Start)
  DECnet/C/dncopy          copy a file from a node
  DECnet/C/dndir           list a directory on a node
  DECnet/C/dnnodes         copy node names from another node (NML)
  DECnet/C/dncheck         test a network card for DECnet
  DECnet/C/dnstartup       adds/removes DECnet in S:User-Startup
  DECnet/L/dn-handler      the DN: file system
  DECnet/S/DECnet-Start    sets up DECnet:, DN: and starts dnetd
  DECnet/S/DECnet-Stop     stops dnetd
  DECnet/Storage/Mountlist mount entry for DN:
  DECnet/Icons             icons DN: shows for itself and the nodes
  LIBS:decnet.library      used by the programs above
  ENVARC:DECnet/nodes      your node list


The node list
-------------
DECnet has no name service, so every node keeps a list of names and
addresses. Yours is ENVARC:DECnet/nodes, one node per line:

  NAME  ADDRESS  [keyword ...]

  USER name      user name for files on that node (omit: none)
  PASSWORD word  password for that user (omit: none)
  NOBLOCK        don't offer DAP blocking when opening files (FOTOFX
                 needs it); directory listings always use blocking
  PURGE          DN: deletes older VMS versions of a file after
                 writing it; without it they are kept
  SHOW           node appears as a drawer in DN:; if no node has
                 SHOW, all nodes appear. Others stay reachable by
                 name (DN:NODE).

For example:

  VAX1    1.10   USER guest PASSWORD secret PURGE SHOW

DN:, dndir, dncopy and dnnodes take USER and PASSWORD from here, DN:
and dncopy also NOBLOCK. USER and PASSWORD given on the command line
win; NOBLOCK counts when it is in either place.

Where to edit: as with all AmigaOS settings, DN: and the programs read
only ENV:DECnet/nodes, the copy in RAM that DECnet-Start makes from
ENVARC:DECnet/nodes at boot.

  - Edit ENVARC:DECnet/nodes to keep a change, then copy it over so
    that it counts at once (or reboot):

      Copy ENVARC:DECnet/nodes ENV:DECnet/nodes

  - Edit ENV:DECnet/nodes only to try something out: it counts at
    once and is gone after the next reboot.

dnnodes ... UPDATE writes new nodes to both files.

To take the names another node knows (like NCP COPY KNOWN NODES):

  dnnodes FROM=area.node REACHABLE UPDATE

REACHABLE leaves out the nodes that node cannot reach right now. New
names are added at the end; existing lines are never changed.


Using it
--------
  List DN:VAX1                      the login directory on VAX1
  Copy DN:VAX1/LOGIN.COM RAM:       copy a file from VAX1
  Copy RAM:test.txt DN:VAX1/        copy a file to VAX1
  dndir VAX1 [GUEST]*.*             directory listing in VMS syntax
  dncopy VAX1 TEST.TXT RAM:test.txt
  dncopy VAX1 [GUEST]TEST.TXT RAM:  into a drawer: keeps the name
  dncopy VAX1 BIG.DAT RAM:big.dat PROGRESS=WINDOW
                                    with a progress window (TEXT: a
                                    progress line in the Shell)

Subdirectories are drawers: DN:VAX1/A/B is [.A.B]. DN: shows its own
disk icon, an icon for every node and, inside a node, an icon for every
file and directory, chosen by the file's extension (text, picture or
other file). These icons are made up by DN:; nothing is written to the
node. Only Workbench sees them in a directory; Shell commands like
"Copy DN:VAX1/#? RAM:", List or file managers do not get them. Icons the Workbench saves there (Information, Snapshot, dragging
a file) are dropped. To give a file a real icon on the node, copy one
in the Shell:

  Copy MyIcon.info DN:VAX1/PICTURE.IFF.info

The icons DN: uses are in DECnet/Icons; you may replace them.

dnetd writes its messages to T:dnetd.log. If it does not start (wrong
driver or unit, for example), DECnet-Start says so and shows the log.
DECnet-Start returns 0 when dnetd runs and 5 (WARN) when not, so a
script that runs it can test "If WARN"; the boot goes on either way.
"dnetd CHECK" tells the same at any time (return code 0, otherwise 5);
DECnet-Stop, or "dnetd STOP", ends it.


Network cards
-------------
dncheck tells whether a card's driver can carry DECnet. Tested so far
(dncheck, DN: and dncopy, 7 MB read and written back unchanged):

  Card                  Driver                          Result
  A2065                 a2065.device 2.16a (Aminet)     works, also on
                                                        a real A3000
  Hydra AmigaNet        hydra.device 1.44 (Aminet)      works
  X-Surf                x-surf.device 1.16 (iComp)      works
  X-Surf 100            x-surf-100.device 1.16 (iComp)  works
  Ariadne II            ariadne_ii.device 43.12         works
  3Com 3C589B PCMCIA    3c589.device 1.5 (Aminet)       works on real
                                                        A1200s (10BASE-T,
                                                        OS 3.2.3, 3.9)
  NE2000 PCMCIA         cnet.device 1.9 (Aminet)        not tested yet
  Zorro-LAN-IDE         its own SANA-II driver          not tested yet

Except for the real A3000 and A1200, these tests ran in the emulator
Amiberry (TAP networking). The driver code is the same as on real
hardware, but the card is emulated; reports from real cards are
welcome. The 3C589B was checked with dncheck and DN:; a tester also
fetched the node list with dnnodes and logged in to a VAX with
VAXterm (after dntermpatch, DECnet-Term package). On the
A1200 it opens only after CardReset (Aminet util/boot/CardReset.lha),
even after power-on: put "CardReset" into S:User-Startup before
DECnet-Start. Its BNC port did not send on our card. cnet.device
keeps its own Ethernet address, so dnetd would have to use its
promiscuous fallback (dncheck says so).


Version history
---------------
0.2 (04.11.2026)
  - ReadMe: how to move Miami, or other network software started from
    S:Startup-Sequence, below the User-Startup call
  - ReadMe: the 3Com 3C589B works on real A1200s (OS 3.2.3 and 3.9,
    with CardReset); Zorro-LAN-IDE listed as not tested yet
  - DECnet-Start: if dnetd cannot start (e.g. the PCMCIA card is
    missing) or DN: cannot be mounted, the boot goes on (it stopped
    before LoadWB); it returns 5 (WARN) when dnetd does not run
  - Installer: if "Start" cannot start dnetd, it says so and points to
    T:dnetd.log (it went on and offered to copy node names)
  - Remove deletes only DECnet's own files (it deleted the whole
    DECnet drawer, with the user's files in it), and DECnet-Term with
    DEVS:dnterm.device
  - dnstartup 0.2: leaves S:User-Startup alone if the DECnet block has
    no ";END DECnet" line (it removed everything after it), and no
    longer takes other blocks whose name starts with "DECnet"

0.1 (26.10.2026)
  - first release for testers: dnetd 0.8, decnet.library 1.2,
    dn-handler 0.9 (DN:), dncopy 0.3, dndir 0.3, dnnodes 0.4,
    dncheck 0.5, dnstartup 0.1
  - tested on AmigaOS 2.04, 3.1 and 3.2.3 in Amiberry and on a
    real A3000


